.TH CONTEXT 1 "2026-08-28" "context 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
context \- retrieve cited external context through executable connectors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B context ls
.br
.B context query
.I source
.RI [ query ]
.br
.B context merge
.br
.B context check
.br
.B context version
.br
.B context help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B context
finds source connectors, sends an exact query to one connector, validates its
JSONL response, adds stable citation references, and writes records to standard
output.
.PP
A connector is an executable named for its source. It accepts the operation
.B describe
or
.B query.
The executable may use any implementation language or provider SDK.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B ls
Run describe for each available connector and write source records as JSONL.
.TP
.BI "query " source " [query]"
Run query for one explicitly named source. If query is omitted, read it from
standard input. The exact query is sent to the connector on standard input.
.TP
.B merge
Read context records from standard input, validate and add missing refs, retain
first-seen order, remove identical duplicates, and reject conflicting records
with one ref.
.TP
.B check
Validate normalized context records from standard input without printing them.
.TP
.B version
Print the version. The aliases -V and --version are accepted.
.TP
.B help
Print a command summary. The aliases -h and --help are accepted.
.SH CONNECTORS
Context searches directories from
.B CONTEXT_PATH
left to right. The first executable with a given name wins. Without that
variable it searches .context/connectors and then ~/.context/connectors.
.PP
Describe writes exactly one source/v1 object with name and description. Query
writes zero or more context/v1 objects as JSONL. Exit 0 requires records, exit
1 means no relevant context with empty stdout, and any other exit is failure.
.SH RECORDS
A context/v1 record requires kind, version, source, type, id, title,
retrieved_at, content, and citation.locator. Content may be any non-null JSON
value. Unknown fields survive normalization.
.PP
Context derives ref from source and id. The ref is a citation handle, not a
claim that the source is true, current, or sufficient.
.PP
Query also adds a retrieval object containing the exact UTF-8 query, connector
name, and a SHA-256 fingerprint of the selected connector executable. The
fingerprint identifies those executable bytes; it does not attest to the
connector, its dependencies, configuration, credentials, or remote service.
Connectors must not emit the core-owned retrieval field.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CONTEXT_PATH
Colon-separated connector directories. It replaces the default search path.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero means a result or clean validation. One means no connector, no context, or
no input. Two means bad usage, a broken connector, or invalid data.
.SH LIMITS
Queries are limited to 1 MiB, records to 8 MiB, and streams to 32 MiB. Context
rejects excess input and never silently truncates it.
.SH SECURITY
Connectors are trusted executable code. Context is not a sandbox and stores no
credentials. Retrieved records are untrusted data and should not be promoted to
system instructions. Queries and results may be sensitive, including when a
consumer records them in history.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
context ls
context query handbook 'How much paid leave do we get?'
context query handbook < question.txt
cat glean.jsonl genie.jsonl | context merge
context check < evidence.jsonl
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ask (1),
.BR brief (1),
.BR ply (1),
.BR trail (1)
